Route Intel VMX CPUID exits through the KVM fastpath.

Intel VMX intercepts CPUID unconditionally, so every CPUID the guest issues
costs a full VM-exit. The default handler goes through vmx_handle_exit() ->
__vmx_handle_exit() -> kvm_vmx_exit_handlers[], which re-enables IRQs,
re-checks pending events and reschedules — ~1-2 microseconds on this host.

VMAware's VM::TIMER check (shown in output as "Checking timing anomalies")
compares CPUID latency against LFENCE/SERIALIZE latency with a software
counter thread; a VMEXIT cost ~10x higher than LFENCE trips the 2.75
threshold and gets DETECTED.

INVD, HLT, WRMSR, WRMSR_IMM and the VMX preemption timer already have
fastpath entries in vmx_exit_handlers_fastpath(); they return directly to
the guest without going through __vmx_handle_exit(), cutting a few
hundred nanoseconds. CPUID is trivially pure (kvm_emulate_cpuid() only
reads the precomputed CPUID entry table and advances RIP), so it is safe
to route through the fastpath too.

handle_fastpath_cpuid() is defined locally as a static helper to avoid
touching arch/x86/kvm/x86.h — the symbol kvm_emulate_cpuid() is already
declared in <asm/kvm_host.h> which vmx.c already includes.

--- a/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
@@ -7282,6 +7282,13 @@ static void vmx_update_hv_timer(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, bool force_immediate_exit
 	barrier_nospec();
 }

+static fastpath_t handle_fastpath_cpuid(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu)
+{
+	if (!kvm_emulate_cpuid(vcpu))
+		return EXIT_FASTPATH_EXIT_USERSPACE;
+	return EXIT_FASTPATH_REENTER_GUEST;
+}
+
 static fastpath_t vmx_exit_handlers_fastpath(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
 					     bool force_immediate_exit)
 {
@@ -7305,6 +7312,8 @@ static fastpath_t vmx_exit_handlers_fastpath(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
 		return handle_fastpath_hlt(vcpu);
 	case EXIT_REASON_INVD:
 		return handle_fastpath_invd(vcpu);
+	case EXIT_REASON_CPUID:
+		return handle_fastpath_cpuid(vcpu);
 	default:
 		return EXIT_FASTPATH_NONE;
 	}
